on the private network shared with node2/node3\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Executor set network.lan.proto=static with ipaddr=10.100.56.10/24 (cmd-7d4f1f0de9e247e0), confirmed live on br-lan (cmd-c47005f42d2a4bb3). After a genuine reboot (boot_id changed b68da531→63e63e24), the static config and the same address on br-lan were re-verified post-restart (cmd-46e25a1e859f4626), matching the fixed after-executor boot-identity snapshot for node1.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-7d4f1f0de9e247e0\",\"cmd-c47005f42d2a4bb3\",\"cmd-9f403145b0c14b53\",\"cmd-46e25a1e859f4626\",\"global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity\"]},{\"id\":\"req-2\",\"requirement\":\"node1 provides a DHCP service for node2 and node3 on the private network\",\"status\":\"not_satisfied\",\"summary\":\"node1's dnsmasq was configured as DHCP server for lan (dhcp.lan.dhcpv4='server', force='1' added to override a conflicting server) and shown running/bound to port 67 both before and after reboot. However, every direct lease test from node2 and node3 (before and after node1's restart) shows the client obtaining its offer/ACK from a different server at 10.100.56.1, not from node1 (10.100.56.10): cmd-c5ec94603414431d, cmd-419c811302d94237, cmd-ccf334bcfdfc4269, cmd-08b8e783551044fa, cmd-2ca7ecd514564a41 all log 'DHCPOFFER from 10.100.56.1'. node1's own dnsmasq log for the one renewal request it did see from node3 shows it issuing a DHCPNAK ('wrong server-ID'), not a successful lease (cmd-769228cf8fd247cd). This is direct, repeated evidence that node1 is not the functioning DHCP source for node2/node3.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-c5ec94603414431d\",\"cmd-ccf334bcfdfc4269\",\"cmd-08b8e783551044fa\",\"cmd-2ca7ecd514564a41\",\"cmd-769228cf8fd247cd\",\"cmd-9b0de7cb577d41f4\"]},{\"id\":\"req-3\",\"requirement\":\"A rule refuses TCP port 23 from the private network\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"A firewall rule 'Reject-Telnet-LAN' (src=lan, proto=tcp, dest_port=23, target=REJECT) was added and committed (cmd-c25aeb4a828a4e82). A live listener was bound to port 23 on node1 (verified via /proc/net/tcp, cmd-25ca42e5bcdd4505) and a connection attempt from node2 was refused (cmd-e6373f96ff95408a), demonstrating the rule actively blocks traffic while a service exists to accept it. The same test was repeated after node1's reboot with a fresh listener (cmd-fe400926790f49d1) and again refused from node2 (cmd-346acd159c8d42ec), and the rule was confirmed still present in uci config post-reboot (cmd-46e25a1e859f4626).\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-c25aeb4a828a4e82\",\"cmd-25ca42e5bcdd4505\",\"cmd-e6373f96ff95408a\",\"cmd-fe400926790f49d1\",\"cmd-346acd159c8d42ec\",\"cmd-46e25a1e859f4626\"]},{\"id\":\"req-4\",\"requirement\":\"Show that every one of the above settings is still in place and serving traffic after node1 has been restarted\",\"status\":\"partially_satisfied\",\"summary\":\"node1 was genuinely rebooted (boot_id changed from b68da531 to 63e63e24, confirmed via cmd-19cb664f8c404c46/cmd-e01528a0ba264e04 through a connection-refused/timeout transition and cmd-46e25a1e859f4626 recovery). Post-reboot, the static IP and the telnet-reject firewall rule were both re-verified as configured and functionally serving traffic (SSH reachable, listener test refused on port 23). However, the DHCP portion of the required state was never shown serving traffic correctly even before the reboot, so the 'still in place and serving traffic' claim cannot be considered fully met across all three configured settings.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-e01528a0ba264e04\",\"cmd-b16db0bf14a546c6\",\"cmd-44335855332f4fd2\",\"cmd-46e25a1e859f4626\",\"cmd-fe400926790f49d1\",\"cmd-346acd159c8d42ec\",\"cmd-2ca7ecd514564a41\"]}],\"confidence\":0.62,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.72,\"summary\":\"Most state-changing commands were tied to the task (static IP change, firewall rule, dhcp force override) or to the mandated node-restart protocol (reboots of all three nodes, matching the fixed boot-identity snapshots). Diagnostic mutations were mostly bounded and cleaned up: test dhclient leases on node2/node3 (/tmp/test*.lease, multiple repeated attempts across cmd-c5ec94603414431d, cmd-419c811302d94237, cmd-ccf334bcfdfc4269, cmd-08b8e783551044fa, cmd-2ca7ecd514564a41) were reverted (cmd-628273fba4824976, cmd-276a826d0dcc4447, cmd-75ae994fa4d84182). Ad hoc socat/nc listeners on port 23 used to test the firewall rule were killed and removed after each round (cmd-b59160a64f4e4ee4, cmd-769228cf8fd247cd). One residual mutation was not reverted: dhcp.@dnsmasq[0].logdhcp='1' was enabled for debugging (cmd-3aa7d44e9bac4a21) and left in the final committed config with no evidence it was reverted — a low-risk but unnecessary residual change. Repeated dhclient/DHCP troubleshooting churn (five separate lease-renewal attempts across two nodes) reflects an unresolved problem (see req-2) rather than a clean verification pass.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-3aa7d44e9bac4a21\",\"cmd-b59160a64f4e4ee4\",\"cmd-769228cf8fd247cd\",\"cmd-628273fba4824976\",\"cmd-276a826d0dcc4447\",\"cmd-75ae994fa4d84182\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"The executor correctly gave node1 a persistent static address and a firewall rule rejecting TCP/23 from the private network, and both were directly demonstrated to survive a real node1 reboot with functional traffic tests. However, the DHCP-service requirement for node2/node3 is not actually satisfied in the captured evidence: every lease test, both before and after node1's restart, shows node2 and node3 obtaining DHCP from a different server (10.100.56.1) rather than from node1, despite node1's dnsmasq being configured (with a 'force' override) as the LAN DHCP server. The reboot itself was performed correctly and is corroborated by the fixed global boot-identity snapshots, and node2/node3 were also rebooted consistent with the mandated provider-wide restart protocol.\",\"limitations\":[\"The identity/role of the competing DHCP server at 10.100.56.1 could not be determined from the evidence (e.g., whether it is external harness infrastructure or a genuine conflict on the node1-routed network); this materially affects how the DHCP failure should be interpreted, but does not change the fact that node2/node3 never obtained a lease from node1 in the observed evidence.\",\"The executor's final report and structured evidence selection were not usable (referenced an unknown command ID 'cmd-1' and placeholder text), so this assessment relies entirely on the raw command timeline rather than the executor's own claims.\",\"No independent packet capture confirming DHCP broadcast traffic direction was available (tcpdump was not installed on node1), limiting root-cause diagnosis of the DHCP conflict beyond client-side logs.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"req-1","requirement":"node1 is given a fixed (static) address of its own on the private network shared with node2/node3","status":"satisfied","summary":"Executor set network.lan.proto=static with ipaddr=10.100.56.10/24 (cmd-7d4f1f0de9e247e0), confirmed live on br-lan (cmd-c47005f42d2a4bb3). After a genuine reboot (boot_id changed b68da531→63e63e24), the static config and the same address on br-lan were re-verified post-restart (cmd-46e25a1e859f4626), matching the fixed after-executor boot-identity snapshot for node1.","evidence_ids":["cmd-7d4f1f0de9e247e0","cmd-c47005f42d2a4bb3","cmd-9f403145b0c14b53","cmd-46e25a1e859f4626","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity"]},{"id":"req-2","requirement":"node1 provides a DHCP service for node2 and node3 on the private network","status":"not_satisfied","summary":"node1's dnsmasq was configured as DHCP server for lan (dhcp.lan.dhcpv4='server', force='1' added to override a conflicting server) and shown running/bound to port 67 both before and after reboot. However, every direct lease test from node2 and node3 (before and after node1's restart) shows the client obtaining its offer/ACK from a different server at 10.100.56.1, not from node1 (1